<TopicPage slug="subjects/peace-conflict-studies/foundational/what-are-peace-and-violence">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Peace and conflict studies opens with definitional surgery on two words everyone thinks they
    understand. Johan Galtung's framework — negative vs positive peace; direct, structural and
    cultural violence — gives the field its analytical spine, and its most radical implication:
    a country without gunfire can still be violent, if its arrangements quietly shorten lives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Galtung's pyramid: peace, direct, structural, cultural" widget="equationStep" config={galtungCfg} />

  <h2>Why the distinctions matter</h2>
  <p>
    If peace is merely the absence of shooting, then any ceasefire counts as success and its
    collapse is mysterious. If peace requires positive conditions — fair institutions, unblocked
    opportunity — then a ceasefire is only the beginning, and the field's job is to study what
    those conditions are. The definitions are not academic; they decide what gets funded and
    measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Structural violence, made concrete</h2>
  <p>
    Galtung's example remains the clearest: two people die of the same treatable pneumonia — one
    in a country with clinics, one without. No actor pulled a trigger, but the arrangement
    shortened a life. Epidemiologists now quantify this as "excess mortality"; the field reads it
    as violence-by-structure, and asks who benefits from the arrangement persisting.
  </p>

  <h2>Measurement: the field's empirical turn</h2>
  <p>
    Modern peace research quantifies what it studies — the Global Peace Index's 23 indicators,
    Uppsala's conflict datasets, casualty archives — precisely because "peace" is contested. The
    numbers do not settle the definitions; they discipline them. Later units apply this rigour to
    the causes of war, the methods of peacebuilding, and the justice that follows atrocities.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galtung, "Violence, Peace, and Peace Research" (1969); Barash &amp; Webel, <em>Peace and Conflict Studies</em>; Global Peace Index methodology.</p>
</TopicPage>
